# Every composed layer's own demand, nameplate and draw, as the composer filed them. One row per
# (fusion, quantity) layers/summed_quantities.sqlc has for a layer composition/fusions.sqlc lists.
#
# The composed layer's side of its sum
#   A fusion's sum has two sides: the converted parts, and the figure the composer files on the
#   composed layer itself. This is the second, for every quantity the layer files at all, as a
#   figure, a typed absence or a derivation. composition/derived_fusions.sqlc reads the quantities
#   filed as the fusion's sum, and composition/unstated_quantities.sqlc the ones filed with neither
#   a figure nor a derivation.
#
# One row per pair
#   composition/fusions.sqlc carries `pm.fusion`'s key, one row per composed layer, so the join
#   cannot fan out a quantity. algebra/fusion_quantities.sqlc holds it: every row of
#   layers/summed_quantities.sqlc is a composed layer's or a layer that composes nothing.
-- composition/fusions.sqlc joined to layers/summed_quantities.sqlc on the composed layer.
SELECT f.filing, f.layer, s.quantity, s.low, s.mode, s.high, s.unit, s.absent, s.derivation
FROM      (
    :compose(composition/fusions.sqlc)
) f
JOIN      (
    :compose(layers/summed_quantities.sqlc)
) s ON s.filing = f.filing AND s.layer = f.layer
